<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">James Frazer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1854">1854</a>–<a href="/years/1941">1941</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as James George Frazer</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">James Frazer was a Scottish anthropologist and classical scholar, author of The Golden Bough, first published in 1890. His comparative study of magic, religion and myth argued that many societies had divine kings whose vigour was bound to the fertility of the land and who were killed when they weakened, citing the Shilluk reth among his examples. The work influenced anthropology and modern literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-frazer.jpg" alt="James Frazer" width="240" height="300" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Contemporary photograph · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JamesGeorgeFrazer.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Frazer was born on 1 January 1854 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a>, the son of Katherine Brown and Daniel F. Frazer, a chemist. He studied at the University of Glasgow and at Trinity College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, where he graduated with honours in classics and remained a Classics Fellow all his life. He went on to study law at the Middle Temple but never practised. He was knighted in 1914. In 1896 he married Elizabeth Grove, known as Lilly, a writer who became his manager and publicist and arranged translations of his work into French.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Frazer's interest in social anthropology was aroused by E. B. Tylor's Primitive Culture of 1871 and encouraged by his friend, the biblical scholar William Robertson Smith. Except for visits to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> he did not travel widely; his data came from ancient histories and from questionnaires mailed to missionaries and imperial officials around the world. He was the first scholar to describe in detail the relations between myths and rituals.</p><p>The Golden Bough appeared in two volumes in 1890, in three in 1900, and in a third edition of twelve volumes finished in 1915, with a thirteenth added in 1936. Lilly Frazer compiled an abridgement in 1922. In the third edition Frazer proposed that cultures pass from magic to religion to science. He saw magic and science as alike in their stress on experiment, and religion as belief in personal supernatural forces that are to be appeased. He also wrote a six-volume commentary on Pausanias's description of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, which scholars still value.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Golden Bough reached beyond academia. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigmund-freud">Sigmund Freud</a> cited Frazer's Totemism and Exogamy in Totem and Taboo, and the cycle of life, death and rebirth that Frazer saw behind many peoples' myths captivated artists and poets; the most notable product was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/t-s-eliot">T. S. Eliot</a>'s poem The Waste Land of 1922. Frazer's vision of the annual sacrifice of a Year-King has not been borne out by field studies.</p><p>Late-20th-century scholars criticised the work. Edmund Leach objected to its comparisons between widely separated cultures, and the historian Timothy Larsen argued that Frazer applied Christian terms such as baptism to non-Christian cultures. More recently the book has been criticised for assuming that European peasants, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aboriginal-australians">Aboriginal Australians</a> and Africans represented earlier stages of cultural evolution. Frazer and his wife died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a> within a few hours of each other on 7 May 1941.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “James George Frazer” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The reth was a sacred king, in whom the spirit of Nyikang was believed to live; his health was linked to the wellbeing of the land, a case made famous by <b>James Frazer</b> and E. E. Evans-Pritchard.</blockquote><a href="/state/shilluk-kingdom">Shilluk Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.86,-4.25&amp;year=1910" aria-label="Glasgow on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4457 260 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of James Frazer"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4907 530h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4907 530h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.86,-4.25&amp;year=1910">What was here in 1910? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
